The demo shows these credits in the UI whenever a track is AUDIBLE: the credit
is raised by the sound starting, not by the track being picked, so it appears
with the music however long a browser holds the sound back. If you remove that
credit deck, remove the tracks with it. The library itself (src, dist) ships no
audio.

The demo also bundles five short speech clips that drive the talking face. They
are NASA recordings, works of the United States government and therefore in the
public domain: no license to satisfy and no attribution required. The source
item says the same thing in its own metadata, where it is marked with the
Creative Commons Public Domain Mark 1.0. They are trimmed, loudness normalized,
downmixed to mono and re-encoded here, and each one still carries NASA in its
own tags and the source recording's filename as its title tag, so this claim can
be checked against the file itself rather than taken on trust.

  voice-briefing.mp3, voice-countdown.mp3, voice-hold.mp3, voice-recorders.mp3,
  voice-sendoff.mp3
